A(('p', '4.3 What Jesus does not name in the stories. Jesus names joy in heaven, the angels of God, one sinner that repenteth, and ninety and nine just persons which need no repentance. He leaves the parts of the stories without an identification. Jesus does not identify the man who has the sheep. Jesus does not identify the sheep that is lost. Jesus does not identify the ninety and nine that are left. Jesus does not identify the wilderness. Jesus does not identify the friends and neighbours. Jesus does not identify the woman. Jesus does not identify the ten pieces of silver, the one piece that is lost, the candle or the house. Jesus does not identify the nine pieces that are not lost. Jesus does not state in so many words that the lost sheep is the sinner of 15:7, or that the ninety and nine sheep are the just persons of 15:7. He says “likewise,” and then names the sinner and the just persons. Jesus does not state that the friends and neighbours of 15:6 and 15:9 are the angels of 15:10, and He does not state what the “heaven” of 15:7 is beyond the words “in heaven.”'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'Because Jesus names only joy in heaven, the angels of God, one sinner that repenteth and ninety and nine just persons which need no repentance, nothing further is named here. A reader who says that the man is a certain person, that the sheep is a certain people, that the ninety and nine are a certain company, that the woman or the coin stands for something, or that the friends are certain persons is supplying what the Lord did not.'))
A(('p', '4.4 What Jesus does not name in Matthew. Jesus does not identify the man who has the sheep, the sheep that is gone astray or the mountains. He does not say in so many words that the sheep is “one of these little ones”; He says “Even so,” and then names them. The phrase “these little ones” also stands in 18:10, “Take heed that ye despise not one of these little ones,” and Jesus does not say in 18:14 that it names the same persons in the same way as in 18:10. Each verse is read as written. Jesus also does not state who “the ninety and nine which went not astray” are.'))
A(('p', '4.5 The explanations stay with their accounts. The words of Luke 15:7 and 15:10 belong to Luke’s stories, and the words of Matthew 18:13–14 belong to Matthew’s. The shared sheep, the shared numbers and the shared word “rejoice” do not make the accounts one, and neither account’s words supply the identifications the other leaves out. The “lost sheep of the house of Israel” of Matthew 10:6 and 15:24 are Jesus’ words in other settings, and Jesus does not join them to the sheep of Luke 15 or of Matthew 18.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'This section states the meaning that arises from the comparisons themselves. It repeats none of Jesus’ identifications from Section 4 and assigns an identity to no part of the stories.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison of the sheep. In Luke the comparison is put as a question to the hearers: what man of them, with a hundred sheep and one lost, would not go after the lost one until he found it? The meaning of the comparison is in the movement of the story: a loss, a search that does not stop, a finding and a joy that is shared.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the sheep comparison communicates.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The one is sought.** A man with a hundred goes after the one that is lost. The comparison puts the lost one before the whole number that is safe.',
    '**The search lasts until the finding.** The man goes “until he find it.” The story does not stop the search part way.',
    '**The finding is carried with joy.** The man lays it on his shoulders, “rejoicing,” and the story does not describe a rebuke or a delay.',
    '**The joy is shared.** The man calls together friends and neighbours and asks them to rejoice with him. The finding is not kept private.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the coin comparison communicates. The second comparison repeats the movement with a different seeker and a different thing lost. A woman with ten pieces loses one. She lights a candle, sweeps the house and seeks diligently. The effort shows how much the one piece is sought, and the story again ends in a calling together and a shared rejoicing.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The seeking is thorough.** A candle is lit, the house is swept and the search is diligent. The comparison shows the loss taken seriously.',
    '**The search lasts until the finding.** She seeks “till she find it.”',
    '**The joy is shared again.** The woman calls her friends and her neighbours together and says, “Rejoice with me.”',
    '**The second follows the first.** The coin is joined to the sheep by “Either,” and the movement of the sheep is told again.',
]))
A(('p', '5.4 What Matthew’s comparison communicates. Matthew puts the same kind of question, “How think ye?”, and gives a smaller story: one gone astray, a search, a finding, a rejoicing. The weight in Matthew falls on the one gone astray and on the Father’s will that Jesus states in His own closing sentence (Section 4). Matthew’s comparison stands after the question of greatness and the child, and what it communicates is read there.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers in Luke were those who drew near to hear and those who murmured that Jesus received sinners and ate with them (15:1–2). They were meant to understand, from the stories, that in the ministry Jesus was carrying out, a lost one is sought until found and the finding is a cause for rejoicing shared with others. The hearers in Matthew were the disciples who had asked who is greatest in the kingdom of heaven (18:1), and they were meant to understand that the one gone astray is sought and that its loss is not the Father’s will. These are comparisons about that Kingdom ministry, and they are not about the church of the Body.'))
A(('p', '5.6 What this section does not do. It does not say who the man or the woman is, or what the sheep, the coin, the wilderness, the candle or the house is. It does not say who the friends and neighbours are. It does not turn seeking, finding, rejoicing or repenting into steps by which a person is saved. It draws no meaning from another parable into these stories.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'The sheep and the coin belong to Luke 15, and the sheep also stands in Matthew 18. They are kept apart here from three neighbors.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Luke 15:11–32', '“A certain man had two sons”', 'The Lost Son and the Elder Brother. The next story in the same chapter, with a father and two sons. It has its own study. The sheep and the coin are not read into it and it is not read into them.'],
    ['Matt 18:21–35', '“Lord, how oft shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him?”', 'The Unforgiving Servant. Peter’s question about forgiveness and a story of a debt, in the same chapter as Matthew’s sheep, with its own setting and its own study.'],
    ['Luke 7:41–42', '“a certain creditor which had two debtors”', 'The Two Debtors. Told in a Pharisee’s house about a creditor and two debtors. It is not a story of a search or of a rejoicing.'],
], [0.17, 0.33, 0.50]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. Luke’s sheep and coin are told after a drawing near and a murmuring (15:1–2). Matthew’s sheep is told after a question about greatness and a child set in the midst (18:1–2) and after Jesus’ words about “these little ones” (18:10). The story that follows in Luke 15 begins, “And he said, A certain man had two sons” (15:11). Matthew’s sheep is followed by a word about a brother who trespasses (18:15), and the story of the debt comes later in the chapter (18:21–35). Each is read in its own place.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. The sheep story has a man, a sheep, ninety and nine and, in Luke, friends and neighbours. The coin story has a woman, a piece of silver, nine other pieces and friends and neighbours. Matthew’s sheep story has a man, a sheep and ninety and nine. The story that follows in Luke 15 has other characters, and the debt stories have creditors, debtors, a king and servants. No character is carried from one account to another.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The sheep and the coin move from loss to search to finding to rejoicing. Matthew’s sheep moves from straying to seeking to rejoicing, and ends with the Father’s will. The Unforgiving Servant moves from a question about forgiveness to a debt. The Two Debtors moves from a debt to a question about love. None of those movements is read into the sheep or the coin.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Three details must not be carried across. First, “my sheep which was lost” in Luke 15:6 is not “the lost sheep of the house of Israel” of Matthew 10:6 and 15:24. Those are other sayings in other settings, and Jesus does not join them. Second, “ninety and nine just persons, which need no repentance” in Luke 15:7 and “the ninety and nine which went not astray” in Matthew 18:13 are different wordings in different accounts, and neither is used to explain the other. Third, the friends and neighbours of Luke 15:6 and 15:9 are not the angels of 15:10, and none of them belongs in Matthew’s account.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. In Luke, Jesus speaks to those who drew near to hear Him and to the Pharisees and scribes who murmured (15:1–2). In Matthew, He speaks to the disciples who asked about greatness in the kingdom of heaven (18:1). Both belong to the earthly ministry in which He said He was sent to preach the kingdom of God (Luke 4:43) and preached that the kingdom of heaven is at hand (Matthew 4:17).'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. The Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The sheep and the coin were told before any of that, and they are not read as though the church of the Body were already present.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Nothing in Luke 15 or Matthew 18 speaks of the Body of Christ, and neither passage is read as though the Body were already formed. The Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul is read in Paul’s letters.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The sheep, the coin, the search, the finding and the rejoicing are not a description of the Body of Christ. The stories are not read as a universal picture of how any person is found by God. The repentance Jesus names in Luke 15:7 and 15:10 is spoken in His earthly ministry, and it is not carried over as a requirement for the Body. The seeking, the finding and the repenting are actions in stories and in sayings, and they are not a mechanism of salvation. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in these stories: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9). Joy in heaven over one sinner is not the Body’s promise, and the stories are not read as the Body’s destiny.'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the comparisons show. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**A lesson of worth.** A man goes after one lost out of a hundred, and a woman searches for one of ten. A reader learns to count the one who is lost as worth the search.',
    '**A lesson of diligence.** The man goes “until he find it,” and the woman seeks “diligently till she find it.” A reader learns not to give up a search that the story does not give up.',
    '**A lesson of shared joy.** Both call others to rejoice when what was lost is found. A reader learns that such joy is not kept to oneself.',
    '**A lesson of weighing a response.** The murmuring of Luke 15:2 is set beside stories of searching and rejoicing. A reader learns to weigh his own response to a lost one who is found against the response these stories describe.',
    '**A lesson of care.** Jesus says, “Take heed that ye despise not one of these little ones” (Matthew 18:10). A reader learns to take heed how he treats the one who is easily overlooked.',
]))
A(('p', 'None of these lessons says that the Body is the sheep, that the Body is the coin, or that the Body is the company that rejoices. They are principles drawn from what the stories show.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Jesus tells of a man with a hundred sheep who loses one, leaves the ninety and nine in the wilderness, goes after the lost one until he finds it, lays it on his shoulders rejoicing, and calls together his friends and neighbours to rejoice with him. He tells of a woman with ten pieces of silver who loses one, lights a candle, sweeps the house and seeks diligently till she finds it, and then calls her friends and her neighbours together to rejoice. After each He says in His own words that there is joy in heaven and in the presence of the angels of God over one sinner that repenteth (Luke 15:7, 10). Matthew gives the sheep again in another setting, after the disciples’ question about greatness and a child set in the midst, and Jesus closes it by saying it is not the will of the Father that one of these little ones should perish (18:14). Jesus names nothing else in the stories. He spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says who the man, the sheep, the woman or the coin is, or that makes seeking, finding or repenting a way of being saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('p', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a loss, a search until the finding, a finding with joy, and a calling together to rejoice, told of a sheep and then of a coin; Matthew tells the sheep again, shorter, and closes with the Father’s will.',
    '**The meaning:** the one that is lost is sought until it is found, and the finding is a cause for joy that is shared.',
    '**The boundaries:** only joy in heaven, the angels of God, one sinner that repenteth and ninety and nine just persons are named; the man, the sheep, the woman, the coin, the wilderness and the friends are not.',
    '**The present lesson:** worth, diligence, shared joy, a weighed response and care, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

